1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,498
"git pull worked for everyone yesterday.
Today it dies with not our ref."

2
00:00:08,838 --> 00:00:14,117
It dies for everyone except one person. That
person improved the shared library from

3
00:00:14,157 --> 00:00:18,866
inside the service, committed in both
repositories, and pushed the service.

4
00:00:18,906 --> 00:00:23,741
The push was accepted. Nothing warned
them. And the service on the server now

5
00:00:23,781 --> 00:00:28,617
records a library commit that exists in
exactly one place in the world: under

6
00:00:28,657 --> 00:00:30,960
.git/modules on that person's laptop.

7
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:36,355
If the laptop goes on holiday before anyone
works out what happened, the whole team

8
00:00:36,395 --> 00:00:39,486
is blocked, and so is CI.
Keep that laptop in mind.

9
00:00:41,326 --> 00:00:44,994
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

10
00:00:45,034 --> 00:00:50,039
A quick reminder first. A submodule is
another repository checked out inside yours.

11
00:00:50,079 --> 00:00:55,144
The superproject is the repository that
contains it, and a gitlink is the entry that

12
00:00:55,184 --> 00:01:00,067
records the one commit it expects. Two
videos ago you learned what a superproject

13
00:01:00,107 --> 00:01:01,648
records about a submodule.

14
00:01:01,988 --> 00:01:06,430
In the last one you watched the recorded
commit and the checked-out commit drift

15
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:11,136
apart on a teammate's machine. Today you
add the third value: the commits that exist

16
00:01:11,176 --> 00:01:15,562
in the submodule's shared repository. And
you go through the remaining standard

17
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:19,652
failures, one after another, each with
its diagnosis, fix and prevention.

18
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:24,903
There are six: the push order, a dirty
submodule, switching to a commit without the

19
00:01:24,943 --> 00:01:29,138
submodule, a conflict between two
pointers, removal, and a changed URL.

20
00:01:29,178 --> 00:01:33,970
The video ends with what all of this means
for a CI job, where nobody is there to

21
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:34,924
read git status.

22
00:01:36,764 --> 00:01:39,854
diagnose not our ref and
prevent it with a push guard;

23
00:01:40,194 --> 00:01:42,967
handle a dirty submodule
when switching branches;

24
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:47,663
resolve a conflict between two recorded
pointers, and say when Git resolves it

25
00:01:47,703 --> 00:01:48,001
alone;

26
00:01:48,341 --> 00:01:53,371
remove a submodule completely, re-add one,
and propagate a changed URL to existing

27
00:01:53,411 --> 00:01:53,804
clones;

28
00:01:54,144 --> 00:01:58,609
state what a CI checkout needs for
submodules, as the section gives it.

29
00:02:00,449 --> 00:02:04,807
Push order. A gitlink is only an ID,
and the server doesn't check it.

30
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:10,098
So pushing the superproject alone is accepted
even when the library commit it names

31
00:02:10,138 --> 00:02:14,560
hasn't been pushed. Git has a guard
for this, and it's off by default.

32
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:19,851
🟢 SAFE: git push --recurse-submodules=check
verifies, before anything is sent, that

33
00:02:19,891 --> 00:02:24,696
every submodule commit named by the
commits being pushed is reachable from a

34
00:02:24,736 --> 00:02:27,309
remote-tracking branch of that submodule.

35
00:02:27,649 --> 00:02:33,401
--recurse-submodules=on-demand goes one
step further and pushes the submodules

36
00:02:33,441 --> 00:02:39,192
first. push.recurseSubmodules=check is
the setting to put in every developer's

37
00:02:39,232 --> 00:02:42,681
configuration for a
repository with submodules.

38
00:02:43,021 --> 00:02:47,414
Its limits: check trusts your remote-tracking
branches, so it's satisfied by a

39
00:02:47,454 --> 00:02:51,904
commit that sits on a library branch you
pushed and that someone later deletes.

40
00:02:51,944 --> 00:02:56,394
And on-demand needs push permission on
the library, which consumers often don't

41
00:02:56,434 --> 00:03:01,054
have. In that case the library change goes
through the library's own review first,

42
00:03:01,094 --> 00:03:03,896
and the superproject bumps
the pointer afterwards.

43
00:03:04,236 --> 00:03:08,944
Dirty submodules. A submodule's working
tree can have uncommitted changes of its

44
00:03:08,984 --> 00:03:13,871
own. The superproject reports them and can't
record them: a gitlink can only name a

45
00:03:13,911 --> 00:03:18,679
commit. The work has to be committed inside
the submodule first, on a branch, and

46
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:22,062
then the new commit ID can be
staged in the superproject.

47
00:03:22,402 --> 00:03:27,570
Switching to a commit without the submodule
leaves its directory behind, because Git

48
00:03:27,610 --> 00:03:31,723
won't delete a directory that
contains a repository's working tree.

49
00:03:31,763 --> 00:03:36,559
With recursion, the checkout removes and
restores the submodule's working tree

50
00:03:36,599 --> 00:03:41,766
together with the superproject's files. The
repository itself stays in .git/modules/

51
00:03:41,806 --> 00:03:42,448
either way.

52
00:03:42,788 --> 00:03:46,840
Pointer conflicts. Two branches of
the superproject move the gitlink.

53
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:51,644
What a merge does depends on how the two
library commits relate, and Git can look

54
00:03:51,684 --> 00:03:56,567
that up only in the submodule's repository.
If one is an ancestor of the other, Git

55
00:03:56,607 --> 00:03:59,888
resolves the pointer by itself
and takes the descendant.

56
00:03:59,928 --> 00:04:04,870
If they have diverged, as these two have,
the superproject can't produce the answer.

57
00:04:05,210 --> 00:04:09,918
The correct pointer is a library commit that
contains both lines of development, and

58
00:04:09,958 --> 00:04:11,670
only the library can create it.

59
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:17,415
Removal. There are two different "removals".
🟡 CAUTION: git submodule deinit <path>

60
00:04:17,455 --> 00:04:22,402
is local. It empties the working directory
and removes the section from your

61
00:04:22,442 --> 00:04:26,010
.git/config, and git submodule
update --init undoes it.

62
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:30,209
🟡 CAUTION: git rm <path> removes
the submodule from the project.

63
00:04:30,249 --> 00:04:35,130
It deletes the gitlink and the section
in .gitmodules, and you commit that.

64
00:04:35,470 --> 00:04:40,976
Three leftovers remain: a possibly empty
.gitmodules, the section in .git/config,

65
00:04:41,016 --> 00:04:46,728
and the repository under .git/modules/. Git
keeps the repository on purpose, so that

66
00:04:46,768 --> 00:04:52,411
old commits which still contain the gitlink
can be checked out without a new clone.

67
00:04:52,751 --> 00:04:57,738
A changed URL. git submodule set-url edits
.gitmodules and synchronizes your own

68
00:04:57,778 --> 00:05:02,954
configuration. A teammate's configuration
still has the URL that init copied months

69
00:05:02,994 --> 00:05:03,205
ago.

70
00:05:03,545 --> 00:05:09,284
🟢 SAFE: git submodule sync copies the URLs
from .gitmodules into .git/config and

71
00:05:09,324 --> 00:05:13,112
into the origin remote of
each initialized submodule.

72
00:05:13,452 --> 00:05:18,889
CI. A CI job is a fresh clone made by a
script, so every default of this chapter

73
00:05:18,929 --> 00:05:19,848
applies to it.

74
00:05:20,188 --> 00:05:24,269
When not to use submodules at all
is the question of the next video.

75
00:05:24,309 --> 00:05:29,117
For today: every failure here is a
disagreement between two of three values, and

76
00:05:29,157 --> 00:05:31,117
every fix makes them agree again.

77
00:05:32,957 --> 00:05:37,968
The three values, once more. What your
book cites. What page the reader's copy is

78
00:05:38,008 --> 00:05:40,836
open at. What the bookshop's
edition contains.

79
00:05:41,176 --> 00:05:46,285
Last time, the first two disagreed. Today's
headline failure is the first and third:

80
00:05:46,325 --> 00:05:51,006
your book cites a page that was never
printed. It exists only in the author's

81
00:05:51,046 --> 00:05:56,094
manuscript. Every reader who follows the
citation is told by the bookshop: we don't

82
00:05:56,134 --> 00:05:56,707
have that.

83
00:05:57,047 --> 00:06:01,484
A pointer conflict is two editions of
your book citing two different pages.

84
00:06:01,524 --> 00:06:05,544
If one page is a later revision of
the other, the choice is obvious.

85
00:06:05,584 --> 00:06:10,200
If the two pages were revised independently,
somebody has to write a page that

86
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:13,901
combines them, and that somebody
works at the other publisher.

87
00:06:14,241 --> 00:06:17,553
Where the analogy stops being
useful: a dirty submodule.

88
00:06:17,593 --> 00:06:20,726
Scribbles in the reader's
copy can't be cited at all.

89
00:06:20,766 --> 00:06:22,642
Only printed pages have numbers.

90
00:06:27,482 --> 00:06:32,033
Every row names two things that should
agree. That's the diagnosis method for

91
00:06:32,073 --> 00:06:35,193
submodules: find the two
values, print both, compare.

92
00:06:37,033 --> 00:06:42,105
You improve the library from inside the
service, on the submodule's main, as the

93
00:06:42,145 --> 00:06:43,319
last video advised.

94
00:06:43,659 --> 00:06:48,521
There are now two unpushed commits in two
repositories: 17d7857 in the library, and

95
00:06:48,561 --> 00:06:53,068
a commit in the superproject whose tree
names it. With the guard, the push is

96
00:06:53,108 --> 00:06:54,957
refused before anything is sent.

97
00:06:55,297 --> 00:07:00,295
"The following submodule paths contain
changes that can not be found on any remote."

98
00:07:00,335 --> 00:07:05,152
Now without the guard. Predict: does the
server accept a superproject commit that

99
00:07:05,192 --> 00:07:08,810
names an unpushed library commit?
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

100
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:17,505
Accepted. The server doesn't
check a gitlink. Ravi pulls.

101
00:07:17,845 --> 00:07:22,917
"not our ref" and the full ID. Note what
state the failure left: the fetch of the

102
00:07:22,957 --> 00:07:27,208
superproject succeeded, and the
merge didn't run. Now the diagnosis.

103
00:07:27,248 --> 00:07:32,509
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds: write
down the questions you would ask to find

104
00:07:32,549 --> 00:07:34,402
the missing commit. I'll wait.

105
00:07:39,242 --> 00:07:43,982
Three questions. Which library
commit does the superproject want?

106
00:07:44,022 --> 00:07:50,013
git ls-tree origin/main vendor/ says 17d7857.
Does the library's shared repository

107
00:07:50,053 --> 00:07:56,044
have it? git ls-remote shows main still at
e216665, and no ref leads to the wanted

108
00:07:56,084 --> 00:07:56,559
commit.

109
00:07:56,899 --> 00:08:01,839
Who has it? Not Ravi's clone. The author
of the superproject commit, on a local

110
00:08:01,879 --> 00:08:06,818
branch. That's the laptop from the opening.
So the fix is on the author's side.

111
00:08:14,158 --> 00:08:17,232
The library commit is pushed,
and the teammate's pull works.

112
00:08:17,572 --> 00:08:20,217
With on-demand, Git pushes
the submodule first:

113
00:08:20,557 --> 00:08:23,539
And the setting for every
developer's configuration:

114
00:08:27,679 --> 00:08:32,337
"modified content, untracked content"
means: the submodule's HEAD is the recorded

115
00:08:32,377 --> 00:08:36,978
commit and its working tree isn't clean.
git submodule status therefore shows no

116
00:08:37,018 --> 00:08:37,558
plus sign.

117
00:08:37,898 --> 00:08:42,993
The diff appends -dirty to the ID.
Predict what git commit -am does with it.

118
00:08:43,033 --> 00:08:43,669
I'll wait.

119
00:08:48,509 --> 00:08:52,915
Nothing to commit. If you expected a
commit, that's a fair guess, but there's

120
00:08:52,955 --> 00:08:57,245
nothing for the superproject to stage.
To look into all submodules at once:

121
00:08:57,585 --> 00:09:02,440
The lower-case m in short status is the
superproject's marker for modified content

122
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:03,575
inside a submodule.

123
00:09:03,915 --> 00:09:08,535
Plain update does nothing: the checked-out
commit already is the recorded one.

124
00:09:08,575 --> 00:09:11,283
--force discards the edit
to the tracked file.

125
00:09:11,623 --> 00:09:15,660
"unable to rmdir 'vendor/textsplit':
Directory not empty".

126
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:21,283
The stray vendor/ is untracked on that
commit. A careless git add -A there would

127
00:09:21,323 --> 00:09:24,236
stage it as a new gitlink. With recursion:

128
00:09:24,576 --> 00:09:28,539
The directory is removed and restored
together with the superproject's files.

129
00:09:28,879 --> 00:09:34,301
use-release records the 0.2.0 release.
use-tokens records a feature branch of the

130
00:09:34,341 --> 00:09:37,471
library. The two library
commits have diverged.

131
00:09:37,811 --> 00:09:42,949
Git's hint says it: recursive merging with
submodules supports only trivial cases.

132
00:09:43,289 --> 00:09:48,562
UU on the path, and three stages in the
index, each a gitlink: stage 1 the merge

133
00:09:48,602 --> 00:09:54,141
base's pointer, stage 2 ours, stage 3 theirs.
There are no conflict markers, because

134
00:09:54,181 --> 00:09:59,055
there's no file to put them in. git
submodule status prints a U and zeros.

135
00:09:59,395 --> 00:10:04,269
The hint lists two ways. Merge inside the
submodule yourself, which creates a merge

136
00:10:04,309 --> 00:10:08,886
commit that exists only in your clone
and must be pushed to the library before

137
00:10:08,926 --> 00:10:13,562
anyone else can use it. Or wait for the
library to merge, and then point at the

138
00:10:13,602 --> 00:10:16,048
result. The demonstration does the second.

139
00:10:16,388 --> 00:10:21,191
The library's main now has a merge,
5a1935a, that contains both.

140
00:10:21,531 --> 00:10:25,923
git add on the path stages whatever commit
the submodule has checked out, which

141
00:10:25,963 --> 00:10:30,579
resolves the path. git commit concludes the
merge. And the case Git resolves alone:

142
00:10:30,919 --> 00:10:35,099
When one of the two library commits is
an ancestor of the other, Git takes the

143
00:10:35,139 --> 00:10:36,992
descendant and says so in one line.

144
00:10:37,332 --> 00:10:41,657
That needs both commits to be present
in the submodule's local repository.

145
00:10:41,697 --> 00:10:46,552
The textbook reports, from runs without a
printed transcript, that in a clone where

146
00:10:46,592 --> 00:10:51,330
the submodule isn't checked out the merge
reports a conflict instead, and that -X

147
00:10:51,370 --> 00:10:52,510
theirs doesn't help.

148
00:10:52,850 --> 00:10:58,022
Local only: the directory is cleared, the
status prefix is a minus, and the history

149
00:10:58,062 --> 00:11:00,974
is untouched. Now the
removal from the project.

150
00:11:09,314 --> 00:11:14,309
"delete mode 160000". Look at the
listing: .gitmodules is still there.

151
00:11:14,349 --> 00:11:16,898
And two more leftovers on your disk:

152
00:11:17,238 --> 00:11:21,563
A quick quiz. Someone adds a
submodule under the same name again.

153
00:11:21,603 --> 00:11:24,921
Does Git accept it, or
refuse it? Say it out loud.

154
00:11:29,761 --> 00:11:34,096
Refused: "A git directory for
'vendor/textsplit' is found locally".

155
00:11:34,136 --> 00:11:38,733
To remove a submodule completely from
your clone, delete the leftovers.

156
00:11:39,073 --> 00:11:43,914
History isn't rewritten by any of this.
A checkout of a commit from before the

157
00:11:43,954 --> 00:11:48,670
removal has the gitlink again, and the
directory is empty until the next git

158
00:11:48,710 --> 00:11:50,172
submodule update --init.

159
00:11:50,512 --> 00:11:55,483
One line of .gitmodules changes, and
your own configuration is synchronized.

160
00:11:55,523 --> 00:11:56,208
Ravi pulls.

161
00:11:59,548 --> 00:12:03,990
He has the new .gitmodules. His
configuration still has the old URL.

162
00:12:07,330 --> 00:12:12,782
sync copies the URL into .git/config
and into the submodule's origin remote.

163
00:12:12,822 --> 00:12:15,817
Then update fetches from the new location.

164
00:12:16,157 --> 00:12:21,274
This segment is described from the action's
documentation; nothing was captured.

165
00:12:21,314 --> 00:12:26,560
The textbook states that actions/checkout
doesn't check out submodules unless told

166
00:12:26,600 --> 00:12:29,332
to: its submodules input
defaults to false.

167
00:12:29,672 --> 00:12:33,709
What follows from the mechanics,
in the textbook's five points.

168
00:12:33,749 --> 00:12:39,079
An empty directory isn't an error to Git,
so a job without submodule checkout fails

169
00:12:39,119 --> 00:12:44,450
later and elsewhere. So add an explicit
step such as git submodule status, and fail

170
00:12:44,490 --> 00:12:49,691
if any line starts with a minus or a plus.
The job tests the recorded commit, not

171
00:12:49,731 --> 00:12:51,050
the library's latest.

172
00:12:51,390 --> 00:12:56,268
The push-order failure appears in CI first.
A workflow that recursively checks out

173
00:12:56,308 --> 00:13:01,246
submodules of a pull request from a fork
lets the fork's .gitmodules choose what is

174
00:13:01,286 --> 00:13:06,044
cloned onto the runner. And git archive
doesn't include submodule contents, so a

175
00:13:06,084 --> 00:13:10,482
build that starts from an archive needs
the submodules fetched separately.

176
00:13:12,322 --> 00:13:13,970
Five mistakes to watch for.

177
00:13:14,310 --> 00:13:19,407
Pushing the superproject before the library.
Root cause: a gitlink is only an ID and

178
00:13:19,447 --> 00:13:24,543
the server does not check that the commit
exists in the library's shared repository.

179
00:13:24,883 --> 00:13:29,430
Trying to save work in a dirty submodule
with a commit in the superproject.

180
00:13:29,470 --> 00:13:34,078
Root cause: a gitlink can name only a
commit; uncommitted content inside the

181
00:13:34,118 --> 00:13:35,301
submodule has no ID.

182
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:40,231
Resolving a pointer conflict by picking
one side. Root cause: when the library

183
00:13:40,271 --> 00:13:45,097
commits have diverged, the correct pointer
is a library commit that contains both,

184
00:13:45,137 --> 00:13:47,115
which only the library can create.

185
00:13:47,455 --> 00:13:52,617
git rm and then a failed re-add. Root cause:
the repository under .git/modules/ and

186
00:13:52,657 --> 00:13:55,751
the section in .git/config
are kept after removal.

187
00:13:56,091 --> 00:14:00,468
Changing the URL in .gitmodules
and expecting clones to follow.

188
00:14:00,508 --> 00:14:06,006
Root cause: each clone uses the copy that
init wrote into .git/config until git

189
00:14:06,046 --> 00:14:07,409
submodule sync runs.

190
00:14:09,249 --> 00:14:13,885
Now, out of the lab. A platform team
shares protocol definitions between eight

191
00:14:13,925 --> 00:14:18,621
services through a submodule. On a Thursday,
CI on main of one service fails at

192
00:14:18,661 --> 00:14:23,537
checkout with "not our ref". The engineer
on call doesn't start with the service's

193
00:14:23,577 --> 00:14:23,836
code.

194
00:14:24,176 --> 00:14:29,114
He prints three values. The gitlink that
origin/main records for the submodule path.

195
00:14:29,154 --> 00:14:32,609
The refs of the definitions
repository, with git ls-remote.

196
00:14:32,649 --> 00:14:37,409
And, after a message in the team channel,
the branches in the author's clone that

197
00:14:37,449 --> 00:14:41,793
contain the wanted commit. The first
value appears in neither of the other

198
00:14:41,833 --> 00:14:45,408
repository's refs, and the author
finds it on a local branch.

199
00:14:45,448 --> 00:14:49,200
One push of the definitions
repository fixes every clone and CI.

200
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:54,880
The follow-up is two lines in the team's
setup script: push.recurseSubmodules set to

201
00:14:54,920 --> 00:15:00,196
check, and a CI step that runs git submodule
status and fails on a leading minus or

202
00:15:00,236 --> 00:15:00,516
plus.

203
00:15:02,356 --> 00:15:09,144
Your turn. Do Exercise 15.4, Level 2,
command prediction, "The first character of

204
00:15:09,184 --> 00:15:15,044
git submodule status", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

205
00:15:15,384 --> 00:15:20,265
For each situation the exercise describes,
write the character you expect, space,

206
00:15:20,305 --> 00:15:25,064
minus, plus or U, and the two values that
agree or disagree, before you run the

207
00:15:25,104 --> 00:15:25,550
command.

208
00:15:25,890 --> 00:15:31,309
The challenge is Exercise 15.9, Level 4,
"It works on your machine", in the same

209
00:15:31,349 --> 00:15:31,650
file.

210
00:15:35,490 --> 00:15:40,139
Answer out loud. A strong answer states the
root cause as a disagreement between two

211
00:15:40,179 --> 00:15:43,600
named things, and says where the
missing commit physically is.

212
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:48,177
Each of the three commands answers one
question, and the answer names the question

213
00:15:48,217 --> 00:15:52,587
with the command: what is wanted, whether
the shared repository has it, and who

214
00:15:52,627 --> 00:15:52,866
does.

215
00:15:53,206 --> 00:15:57,089
It says on whose machine the fix
runs, which isn't the teammate's.

216
00:15:57,129 --> 00:16:01,843
For prevention it names the setting,
describes what it checks before a push, and

217
00:16:01,883 --> 00:16:06,004
gives its limits. It may add where
this failure is usually seen first.

218
00:16:07,844 --> 00:16:12,449
Let's land this. Six failures, and one
method: find the two values that should

219
00:16:12,489 --> 00:16:16,974
agree, print both, compare. Here, the
library commit had to reach the shared

220
00:16:17,014 --> 00:16:17,629
repository.

221
00:16:17,969 --> 00:16:19,629
You should now be able to say:

222
00:16:19,969 --> 00:16:25,171
"not our ref" means the superproject
records a library commit that the library's

223
00:16:25,211 --> 00:16:30,020
shared repository does not have;
push.recurseSubmodules=check prevents it.

224
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:34,783
Uncommitted work in a submodule cannot
be recorded by the superproject; it is

225
00:16:34,823 --> 00:16:36,928
committed inside the submodule first.

226
00:16:37,268 --> 00:16:42,031
A pointer conflict is three gitlinks in
the index; Git resolves it alone only when

227
00:16:42,071 --> 00:16:43,788
one commit contains the other.

228
00:16:44,128 --> 00:16:49,719
Removing a submodule leaves .gitmodules,
a config section and a repository under

229
00:16:49,759 --> 00:16:54,645
.git/modules; a changed URL reaches
clones through git submodule sync.

230
00:16:54,985 --> 00:17:00,119
A CI job is a fresh clone with every default
of this chapter, so it has to ask for

231
00:17:00,159 --> 00:17:02,264
submodules and check their status.

232
00:17:04,104 --> 00:17:08,015
Read sections 23.8 to 23.12 of Chapter 23.

233
00:17:08,355 --> 00:17:13,252
Today you diagnosed "not our ref" from
three questions, and that's a real on-call

234
00:17:13,292 --> 00:17:16,543
skill. If six failures felt
like a lot, that's normal.

235
00:17:16,583 --> 00:17:20,566
The table in the diagram section
is your map, so practise with it.

236
00:17:20,606 --> 00:17:25,320
Next time: subtrees, and choosing between
a submodule, a subtree and a package

237
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:29,037
manager. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

238
00:17:29,077 --> 00:17:30,500
See you in the next one.
